  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow...\n\nA few widely scattered showers are moving across the area this \nafternoon as a shortwave disturbance crosses central Nebraska and \ndirects low-level moisture transport into the region. Rainfall \namounts this afternoon will remain light, generally limited to a \ntrace to a few hundredths of an in inch. Persistent cloud cover will \nhold high temperatures in the mid 70s to low 80s. Northwesterly flow \naloft is also bringing a bit of wildfire smoke to the area, with \nsmall concentrations making it the surface.\n\nPrecipitation chances will increase after midnight as another \nshortwave disturbance pivots into the region. Strengthening low-\nlevel moisture transport and a nocturnal LLJ directed into \nsoutheast Nebraska will support another round of scattered \nshowers. Sufficient elevated instability will also be present \nfor a few embedded thunderstorms overnight, particularly across\nsoutheast Nebraska.\n\nThe environment will support efficient rainfall production, with \nPWAT values rising slightly above 2 inches, exceeding the 90th \npercentile of sounding climatology, and warm-cloud depths reaching 4-\n4.5 km. Rainfall totals will depend on where the heavier bands of \nshowers track but amounts of 0.25 to 1 inch are expected across much \nof the area, with isolated totals exceeding 1 inch possible. Latest \nHREF guidance indicates a 40-80% probability of at least 0.50 inch \nand a 30-60% probability of at least 1 inch, with the highest \nprobabilities across southeast Nebraska. \n\nShowers will gradually clear from west to east Friday morning, with \nmost of the area dry by noon. Cloud cover will slowly break up and \nimprove behind the departing precipitation shield, allowing \nafternoon highs to recover into the upper 80s across eastern \nNebraska and mid 70s towards southwest Iowa. \n\nSaturday and Beyond...\n\nMid- to upper-level ridging will amplify and expand northeastward \ninto the region this weekend, resulting in a warming trend. A \ncorridor of 25 to 30 degrees C temperatures at 850 mb will spread \nacross the area, while continued low-level moisture transport and \nevapotranspiration push dewpoints well into the 70s and potentially \nnear 80 degrees across southwest Iowa.\n\nConfidence is high that the weekend will be hot and humid, with \nmodel guidance continuing to trend upward on temperatures. GFS/GEFS \nappear to mix too deeply into the very warm air aloft, producing \nsurface temperatures of 105 to 115 degrees that are likely too high. \nEPS guidance appears more reasonable, with highs generally in the \nupper 90s to around 105 degrees, though it maintains higher \ndewpoints.\n\nThe latest forecast package calls for highs in the mid 90s to around \n100 degrees Saturday, with heat index values from 100 to 105 \ndegrees. Heat is expected to peak Sunday and Monday, when highs \nreach the upper 90s to around 105 degrees and heat index values \nclimb into the 105 to 115 degree range. Heat headlines will likely \nbe needed, especially given the limited overnight relief as low \ntemperatures remain in the mid to upper 70s.\n\nConfidence in Monday's temperatures is somewhat lower as a shortwave \ndisturbance rounds the ridge, potentially weakening it and bringing \ncloud cover/precipitation to the area. Long-range guidance remains \nsplit on the exact timing and track of these disturbances, but a \nfront will likely move southward across the region sometime Monday \ninto Tuesday, shifting low-level flow to northeasterly and steadily \nlowering dewpoints behind the front. The timing of this front will \nbe important, as an earlier passage could reduce peak heat index \nvalues across portions of the area Monday. \n\nAdditional disturbances will move through during the work week, \ngradually weakening the ridge and bringing highs closer to seasonal \nnorms in the upper 80s and low 90s Tuesday and beyond. Periodic PoPs \nof 15-30% remain in place through the work week.",
